\f1\fs22 Eloise had them brought to luxurious dining chairs around a circular, ornate polished timber table, in an impeccably appointed spacious room with a panoramic view over the ridge to the north-east.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Have you any idea,\'94 she started, after they had taken seats at the table with her, \'93how I\'92ve had to negotiate to stop Fridge Force from barging onto my property, demanding to investigate an infraction of the Refrigeration Secrets Act? Luckily, I persuaded them to accept the piece of equipment you lifted from the fridge so they can verify you didn\'92t open it. Who knows how long that will keep them at bay.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The three hid their ruddily embarrassed faces by looking into their laps.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Please tell me you didn\'92t open it,\'94 Eloise asked in a voice so quiet, it was the most threatening thing any of the three had ever heard.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley choked and swallowed. \'93No. I didn\'92t open it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I mean, you\'92re a TRAMPS officer.\'94 Eloise actually sounded flabbergasted. \'93And a smart, honest one at that.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise threw her hands up. \'93Heaven knows, I can handle the government, TRAMPS and even PAPER, but Fridge Force? You know they can disable any stasis or cooling unit, anywhere, any time, for any reason? Sure, I could probably blast their whole religious order back to the dreamtime, but\'97and let me make this knife-edge clear\'97I might never drink chilled champagne again. Tax! Even unauthorised commercial use of ice is an infraction of the Refrigeration Secrets Act.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley tried to process whether Eloise was being ironic.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Actually,\'94 Sarah explained, \'93good champagne is very palatable at room temperature.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'94Shut the tax up,\'94 Eloise snapped then looked a little embarrassed about her outburst.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s my fault,\'94 Kathy pleaded. \'93I was\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise shot her hand up to interrupt. \'93I\'92ll not hear any ill-conceived confessions.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93All I\'92m saying is, Sarah and Hayley did their best to prevent such an infraction.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise was far from mollified. \'93Then Hayley\'97a TRAMPS officer\'97went and did it anyway.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Please, don\'92t turn Kathy and Sarah in,\'94 Hayley bartered. \'93They were asleep when I\'85\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise let the tension hang in the air.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah held out her hand to Eloise. \'93May I borrow your tablet?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise\'92s expression shifted from triumph to perplexity. She glanced at Kathy and Hayley, who looked equally confused. After a moment\'92s hesitation, she slid her tablet across the table.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah took it and began tapping and swiping with deliberate focus.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The silence stretched. Eloise watched Sarah\'92s face, trying to read her intentions. Kathy and Hayley exchanged uncertain glances.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable> Eloise\'92s patience fractured. \'93What are you doing?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Filling out a form,\'94 Sarah said without looking up.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'94What kind of form?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93A Random Allocation Application Form. On my.gov.ul.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The words hit the room like a concussion grenade. Kathy gasped. Hayley\'92s eyes went wide.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise leaned forward, her voice diamond-edged. \'93You\'92re applying for government-matched Random Allocation? You\'92d accept any old government-matched dreg over my son?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah looked up, meeting Eloise\'92s gaze with steel in her own. \'93I\'92d stand a better chance of being treated as more than just a social ornament.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>For a long moment, Eloise simply stared. Then she leaned back in her chair and waved dismissively. \'93Fine. Throw away your life, then. And any support you\'92ll get from me.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah\'92s finger hovered over the screen then tapped. \'93There. All done.\'94 She looked up with the faintest smile. \'93Now, let\'92s negotiate. I\'92ve probably got a day or so while the system churns, during which time I can cancel.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise\'92s tablet immediately binged.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Everyone froze.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise\'92s eyes narrowed. She reached across and took the tablet back, glancing at the screen. Her eyebrows rose fractionally. \'93That was fast. It seems they allocated you in record time.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah\'92s face went pale. \'93Give me that.\'94 She snatched the tablet back and stared at the screen. The colour drained from her cheeks.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'94What?\'94 Kathy asked. \'93Sarah, what\'92s wrong?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah slowly turned the tablet around so everyone could see.\
